  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 118 PM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nAt 1 PM CDT, skies are sunny and temperatures are warming through \nthe 80s. Winds across central and north central South Dakota are \nsoutherly 5 to 15 mph, with some gustiness at times up to around 20 \nmph. Across northeast South Dakota into west central Minnesota, \nwinds are south-southwesterly 15 to 25 mph with gusts well in excess \nof 35 mph; especially in the downslope-favored regions in the lee of \nthe Coteau, where gusts have been between 45 and 55mph. Relative \nhumidity values across central and north central South Dakota are \ncrashing, down below 25 percent. Along and east of the \nMinnesota/South Dakota border, relative humidity values have been \nslower to diminish, given the higher levels of gulf-sourced moisture \nin the mixing layer/PBL.\n\nThere is a cold front analyzed just starting to enter Corson/Dewey \nCounties (far nw corner of the CWA). Short term guidance has this \nfrontal boundary slowly sinking southeast across the CWA from now \nthrough Sunday afternoon. Low forecast confidence in the temperature \nforecast on Sunday, while this frontal passage is occurring. Suffice \nit to say, the southeastern third of forecast zones should be warmer \nthan the northwestern two-thirds of the CWA. Behind this fropa, \ntemperatures will be cold (closer to climo normal) over the entire \nCWA for Monday through Wednesday. Beyond that, the low level thermal \nadvection pattern should change enough to allow some warming over, \nat least, the western half of the CWA for the latter portion of next \nweek.\n\nThe precipitation potential keeps changing from day to day, with \nwhat previously looked like a decent soaking rain early this \nupcoming week, is fizzling out. With the western CONUS upper level \ntrof that's developing, there could certainly be a few transient \nshortwaves bringing short-lived rain chances to the region between \nSunday night and Wednesday. But, ensembles and deterministic \nsolutions are not on the same sheet of music regarding how to \nevolve/break down the Rex block pattern that will develop over the \nnext few days. Amongst the many potential outcomes, there are \ncurrently two scenarios that stand out the most. In one scenario, \nenergy rounding the top of the western CONUS upper level ridge will \nbe sufficient to kick out the closed low over the intermountain \nwest, bringing a period of precipitation potential and cooler \nconditions during the second half of next week. The other scenario \nholds the Rex block in place, keeping this region generally dry and \nwarm from Thursday through Saturday.",
